@page "/docs/accessibility/audit-verdicts"

@* The verdict each finding gets from the pack and .izzy/audit.json, with a free account (AuditVerdicts.cs): the five
   verdicts, accepting a finding, demo pages. *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="audit-verdicts">
    <Lead>
        A tool's findings are not all failures. axe is wrong about some patterns, some findings need a person's
        eyes, and some are yours to accept. With a free account, <Code>izzy audit</Code> gives each finding a
        verdict and its reason.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The five verdicts</Heading>
        <Table Items="Verdicts" Caption="The verdict of a finding" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Verdict" Width="Length.Rem(10)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Means">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What you do">@context!.Do</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            The report groups the findings by verdict, real ones first, and <Code>results.json</Code> has each
            finding's verdict, reason and confidence.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Where a verdict comes from</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy keeps a list of patterns it has looked at: a rule, the markup it fires on, and what was found by
            hand. Your own accepted findings come first. Then a finding that matches a pattern gets that
            pattern's verdict and reason. A finding of
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-checks">izzy's own checks</Anchor> that neither covers gets its
            verdict from what the check measured. A finding that matches no pattern counts as real, with medium confidence.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A verdict is about one finding. It doesn't say the element is accessible, only that this rule's
            complaint about it is right, wrong or open.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Needs a person</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            This verdict is the line between what a tool can settle and what it can't. izzy measured something
            and a person has to judge it: text in a color set on the element itself, where an exception may
            apply, a control that changes only slightly when it takes focus, a part that disappears in forced
            colors and may be decoration. Look at the element on the page. Then fix it, or accept it with your
            reason.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Accept a finding</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Your own decisions go in <Code>.izzy/audit.json</Code> in the project. An accepted finding stays in the
            report with your reason and doesn't fail a build that uses <Code>--fail-on real</Code> or
            <Code>--fail-on new</Code>. <Code>rule</Code> and <Code>reason</Code> are required.
            <Code>selector</Code> is a CSS selector for the element or one it is inside, and <Code>pages</Code> is a
            path, where <Code>*</Code> stands for one part and <Code>**</Code> for any number. A selector
            also finds an element in an iframe of your own site, and what the iframe is inside counts as around
            the element.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="json" Title=".izzy/audit.json">@ProjectFile</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Accepting is a person's decision, written down with a reason. Add <Code>by</Code> and
            <Code>on</Code> to say who and when. It doesn't make a failure
            conform: it records that you know. A coding agent that runs the audit is told never to accept a
            finding by itself.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Demo pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>demoPages</Code> lists pages that show a component several times, such as a style guide. Two
            landmarks with one name are a demo page's finding there and a real one anywhere else. Add
            <Code>examples</Code>, a selector, to count only what is inside an example.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text, string Do);

    private static readonly Row[] Verdicts =
    [
        new("Real", "A failure, or a finding izzy has no reason to excuse.", "Fix it."),
        new("Needs a person", "A tool can't settle it, such as text in a color set on the element itself.", "Look at it, then fix or accept it."),
        new("Accepted", "You accepted it in .izzy/audit.json. The report prints your reason.", "Nothing, while your reason holds."),
        new("Demo page", "Caused by a page that shows a component several times. A page of an app doesn't have it.", "Nothing."),
        new("False positive", "The rule is wrong here, such as low contrast on a part of a disabled control.", "Read the reason once. If it doesn't fit your markup, treat the finding as real."),
    ];

    private const string ProjectFile = """
        {
          "accepted": [
            {
              "rule": "color-contrast",
              "selector": ".brand-wordmark",
              "pages": "/**",
              "reason": "A logotype: WCAG 1.4.3 exempts it",
              "by": "Dana",
              "on": "2026-11-24"
            }
          ],
          "demoPages": [
            { "pages": "/styleguide/**", "examples": ".example" }
          ]
        }
        """;
}
